course: biology
lesson: 245
title: More builders: caddis cases, paper wasps, moles, apes' beds and sand circles
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Look closely at a pond and you may see a tiny tube of stones walking along the bottom: inside is a caddis fly larva wearing a house it glued together itself.
explain: Animals build with whatever they have: wasps chew wood into paper, moles push soil up into molehills, and ants pile pine needles into a warm dome.
explain: A chimpanzee bends branches into a fresh bed every single night, a bit like making your bed but starting with a tree.
explain: Some builders work under water: a male stickleback glues a nest of weed, and a small pufferfish draws a perfect circle in the sand with its fins.
explain: Every building does a job: protection, warmth, a trap for food, a safe bed, or a show to win a mate.
fact: Caddis fly larvae glue sand grains, tiny shells or plant bits together with silk into a case they carry about, which protects their soft bodies from fish; an artist once gave them gold flakes and pearls and they made jewel cases.
fact: Wasps chew wood fibres with spit into paper and build nests of six-sided cells; the paper layers trap air and keep the nest warm, and wasps have done this for millions of years, while people learned to make paper from wood only in the 1800s.
fact: Hornets build big paper nests, but only the young queens survive the winter; in spring each queen starts a new nest alone, and an old nest is not used again.
fact: Moles dig tunnels and push the soil up into molehills; worms fall into the tunnels and the mole patrols them, and it keeps a larder of worms with bitten heads.
fact: Chimpanzees and other great apes bend branches into a fresh sleeping nest each night, off the cold ground and away from leopards, and a study found chimp nests had fewer bugs than human beds.
fact: Orangutans make umbrellas and roofs from big leaves, learning from their mothers, with whom they stay for about 7 or 8 years.
fact: A small Japanese pufferfish makes a round sand pattern about two metres wide with its fins; the male builds it to attract a female, and divers saw these circles for years before anyone knew who made them.
fact: A male stickleback glues weed together with a sticky thread from his kidneys, turns red in spring, guards the eggs and fans water over them to give them oxygen.
fact: Wood ants pile pine needles into domes that can be over a metre high, warmed by the sun and the rotting needles; birds sometimes sit on the nest and let the ants spray acid on their feathers, which may clean pests called parasites off them.
fact: Hedgehogs gather piles of leaves under bushes to hibernate in, and a pile of leaves and a gap in a garden fence help them.
fact: Hummingbird nests are tiny cups of plant fluff held together with spider silk, which stretches as the chicks grow, and a hummingbird egg is about the size of a jelly bean.
exercise: What is a caddis fly larva's case made of? | Sand, shells or plant bits glued with silk | Wax made in its own body, like a bee's | An empty snail shell it finds on the bottom | Paper chewed from wood
exercise: Why does a caddis larva carry its case around? | It protects its soft body from fish | It keeps it warm in the sun on cold days | It is a little box to store its food in | It helps it to float on top of the water
exercise: What did caddis larvae do when an artist gave them gold flakes and pearls? | They built jewel cases from them | They refused to build at all | They ate the gold | They threw the pearls away
exercise: What is a wasp's nest made of? | Paper made by chewing wood with spit | Mud dried hard in the hot summer sun | Wax like a bee's comb | Leaves sewn together with spider silk
exercise: What shape are the cells in a wasp's nest? | Six-sided | Round like balls | Square like boxes | Three-sided
exercise: What happens to a hornets' nest in winter? | Only young queens survive, and the old nest is not used again | All the hornets sleep in it together, safe and warm, until spring | The workers repair it every winter | The queen stays in it for ever and lays eggs again each year
exercise: Why does a mole push up molehills? | To get rid of the soil from its tunnels | To build lookout towers to watch for foxes | To store water for summer | To mark where it buried its food
exercise: How do a mole's tunnels help it to eat? | Worms fall into them and the mole patrols to catch them | Seeds roll down to the mole | Rain washes fish into them | Birds drop food down the holes for the mole when they have spare worms
exercise: Where does a chimpanzee sleep at night? | In a fresh nest of bent branches in a tree | In the same cave every night, year after year | On the bare ground in a heap | Standing up against a tree trunk
exercise: Why do orangutans hold big leaves over their heads? | As umbrellas against the rain | To hide from their mothers | To fan away flies | To carry food home
exercise: Who builds the round sand circles on the sea floor near Japan? | A male pufferfish, to attract a female | Waves crashing on the sand during big storms | A family of crabs | Divers having fun
exercise: How does a male stickleback glue his nest together? | With a sticky thread from his kidneys | With mud from the bank | With spider silk | With his own scales, which he pulls off one by one
exercise: Why does a male stickleback fan water over the eggs? | To give them oxygen | To wash them away | To cool his fins | To feed them
exercise: What keeps a wood ants' nest warm? | The sun on the dome and the rotting needles | A small fire lit by the ants | The ants' fur | Hot water bubbling up from under the ground
exercise: How can you help a hedgehog get ready for winter? | Leave a pile of leaves and a gap in the fence | Bring it indoors to sleep in a box | Give it milk and bread every single night | Sweep all the leaves away
exercise: What holds a hummingbird's nest together? | Spider silk | Mud and spit | String from people's gardens | Sticky pine resin
exercise: How big is a hummingbird egg? | About the size of a jelly bean | About the size of a big hen's egg | About the size of a tennis ball | Even smaller than a grain of sand
test: «molehill on the lawn again, my dad says the mole just builds mountains for fun» What is right? | No, the heaps are soil pushed up from its tunnels | Yes, moles build hills to climb and look around | No, rabbits make molehills | Yes, to show off to other moles
test: «found an old wasp nest in the shed, will they come back to it next yr?» | No, an old nest is not used again | Yes, the same wasps return every spring | Yes, they sleep inside it all winter | Only if you paint it a bright colour
test: «2 chimps, 3 orangutans in the zoo. how many arms then» | 10 | 5 | 20 | 12
test: «4 hedgehogs under the leaves, no sorry 3. how many legs» | 12 | 16 | 9 | 6
test: What would happen to a hummingbird nest if it were glued with hard mud instead of spider silk? | It could not stretch as the chicks grew | It would float away | The eggs would hatch faster than usual | Nothing would change at all
test: «found the hornets nest under the roof, kill the queen in spring and that's the lot?» What is true? | Each surviving young queen starts a new nest alone in spring | Hornets have no queen | The old queen lives on in the old nest for ever | All the hornets survive the winter together, huddled in the nest
test: A study compared chimp nests with human beds. What did it find? | Chimp nests had fewer bugs | Chimp nests were full of fleas | Human beds were always cleaner | Chimps prefer to sleep in human beds
test: «weird circle in the sand, aliens??» a diver says. What made it? | A small male pufferfish using its fins | Aliens landing their round spaceship in the sea | A whale rolling on the sand | The tide going round
test: Why did nobody need to teach wasps to make paper? | Wasps made paper from wood millions of years before people did | People taught wasps how to make it in the 1800s | Wasps copy paper from the old newspapers in people's rubbish bins | Wasps buy paper from paper mills
test: A young orangutan is four years old. Who does it most likely live with? | Its mother, who teaches it nest skills | Alone in its own tree from the day it is born | A big group of male orangutans | Zookeepers only
test: A bird sits on a wood ants' nest and lets ants climb on it. Why might it do that? | The ants' acid may clean parasites off its feathers | It is trying to hatch the ant eggs | It is stuck in the nest and cannot fly away again | It is building its own nest there
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the case constructed by the caddis fly larva? | A portable shelter of particles bound with silk, affording protection from predatory fish | A fixed dwelling cemented firmly to the bed of the pond | A structure of wax secreted from the abdomen | A borrowed shell of a deceased snail, enlarged with mud and pebbles gathered from the stream bed
exam: Identify the principal material employed by wasps in the construction of their nests. | Wood fibres masticated with saliva to form paper | Mud collected from the margins of ponds and streams | Wax secreted in the manner of the honeybee | Leaves joined together with spider silk
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sleeping arrangements of chimpanzees. | A fresh nest of bent branches is constructed in the trees on each night | The same cave is occupied throughout the year by the whole family group | The animals repose upon the bare ground in a heap | No resting place of any description is prepared
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a hummingbird's nest is able to accommodate the growth of the chicks. | It is bound with spider silk, which stretches as the chicks increase in size | It is constructed of rigid mud which is rebuilt daily | The parents enlarge it each morning with additional stones from the river bank | The chicks vacate it within hours of hatching
